<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.chamber.verify</string>

  <!--
    The chaining is `||` + `;`, not `&&`, and that is the whole point of this
    job surviving contact with a real machine.

    `chamber ingest` exits non-zero for reasons that have nothing to do with
    drift: no roots configured yet, or a configured root that has moved or is
    on an unmounted volume (it ingests the remaining roots and still exits 1).
    Under `ingest && verify` any of those silently skips `verify` entirely —
    the scheduled check stops running, and the only trace is a log line that
    looks like an ordinary ingest complaint. A drift check that quietly stops
    checking is worse than no drift check, because the silence reads as health.

    So `verify` runs unconditionally, and the ingest failure is announced on
    its own line instead of being inferred from an absence.

    The job's exit status is therefore `verify`'s, which preserves verify's
    contract exactly: non-zero iff a belief has no verified support left. If
    ingest's status were folded in, exit 1 would mean either "your evidence
    moved" or "a disk wasn't mounted" — two findings that call for completely
    different responses, collapsed into one indistinguishable number.

    The leading `date` line matters because launchd appends to this log
    forever. Without it, today's run and last month's run are the same
    undifferentiated text.

    The run records the database it checked and verify's exit code, because a
    log that omits both cannot answer the only two questions worth asking of
    it: which corpus was this, and did it pass. An operator reading a wall of
    belief output otherwise has to infer the verdict from the prose.

    Drift raises a notification; a clean run raises nothing. This check
    correctly reports nothing on almost every day, so a log file the operator
    must remember to open is a check they will stop reading. Silence stays
    silent and drift interrupts exactly once. `exit $rc` is load-bearing: an
    `if` whose condition fails would otherwise leave the script exiting 0 and
    quietly destroy verify's exit contract.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>echo "=== chamber $(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z') ==="
chamber config show 2>/dev/null | grep database
chamber ingest || echo "!! ingest FAILED (exit $?) - verifying against the corpus as it stands, which may be stale"
chamber verify
rc=$?
echo "=== verify exit $rc ==="
if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
  msg="A belief has lost its evidence (verify exit $rc). See Library/Logs/chamber-verify.log"
  osascript -e "display notification \"$msg\" with title \"Chamber: evidence drift\"" >/dev/null
fi
exit $rc</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    The interpreter that runs the MiniLM embedder, named explicitly.

    `sh -lc` is a *login* shell, and on macOS a login shell finds
    /usr/bin/python3 — which has no numpy and no onnxruntime. embedMinilm
    therefore threw, the embedder fell back to non-semantic hash vectors, and
    this job re-embedded the entire corpus with them every morning while
    exiting 0. `chamber ask` then answered "nothing in the corpus matches" for
    material that was sitting in the index.

    PATH is the thing that differs between an interactive shell and this one,
    so a setting that depends on PATH cannot fix it. Point this at an
    interpreter that has the dependencies.
  -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>CHAMBER_PYTHON</key>
    <string>REPLACE_WITH_HOME/frida-env/bin/python3</string>
  </dict>

  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>8</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer>
  </dict>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>REPLACE_WITH_HOME/Library/Logs/chamber-verify.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>REPLACE_WITH_HOME/Library/Logs/chamber-verify.log</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
